<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>E7-E8.1 Overview</head><p>State fish and wildlife agencies are responsible for development of their own Wildlife Ac�on Plans and they share its implementa�on. The goals of the plans cannot be achieved without coordinated ac�on undertaken in partnership with others. Working with partners elevates conserva�on to a broader landscape scale, which avoids imposing poli�cal boundaries on natural systems. It also ensures that issues are considered from mul�ple perspec�ves. Addi�onally, it allows states to leverage diminishing resources to undertake conserva�on ac�ons through effec�ve partnerships.</p><p>The WWAP represents an important piece of the Department's overall effort of fish, wildlife, and habitat conserva�on and management. As such, we strive to gather and consider the views of the people who know and care for our state's biodiversity while also mee�ng federal State Wildlife Grant program requirements for the Plan. Wisconsin's approach to public involvement has been to engage people and organiza�ons for development of each itera�on of the WWAP. Addi�onally, Wisconsin aims to implement the priori�es and objec�ves that result from it.</p><p>For this revision of the WWAP, public par�cipa�on is defined broadly to include all input for the plan revision. This includes non-technical and technical input, and tradi�onal and scien�fic ecological knowledge about the major WWAP elements. It includes federal and state agency representa�ves, DNR staff, stakeholders, landowners, tribal na�ons, individuals and organiza�ons that have an interest in the goals of the WWAP. As this implies, because of the</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>E7-E8.2 Approach and Changes from Previous Plan</head><p>The 2025 WWAP, like the 2015 WWAP, combines agency coordina�on (Element 7) and public par�cipa�on (Element 8) into this single sec�on. S�ll, "public" communica�ons and input about the WWAP revision and its implementa�on can generally be dis�nguished as technical (e.g., knowledge about species abundance or distribu�on) and non-technical.</p><p>Past updates to the WWAP relied on commonly recognized methods of ge�ng input from the public -for example, public mee�ngs and open houses throughout the state. The 2015 plan used "Conserva�on Cafés" and an online survey. The Conserva�on Cafes were public mee�ngs held around the state that were casual, collabora�ve and open to a diverse group of partners. The survey was used to beter understand the public priori�es and expecta�ons for the WWAP. These methods did not reach the desired diversity of individuals and organiza�ons or result in plen�ful feedback. Scheduling these opportuni�es or events compresses public par�cipa�on into a �meframe that is not necessarily convenient or even feasible for interested par�es. As a result, we shi�ed our approach for the 2025 WWAP to rely primarily on digital communica�on, online opportuni�es with small mee�ngs and conversa�ons as needed, as opposed to broad group mee�ngs or workshops. The same will be applied to implementa�on. Dra�s of the major elements of the WWAP of the greatest interest to the public [i.e., Ranks, Species of Greatest Conserva�on Need (SGCN), Species of Greatest Informa�on Need (SGIN), associa�on scores and threats and ac�ons reports] were posted on the webpage and available for review throughout the revision period. This approach allows us to use limited resources to engage specific organiza�ons, tribes and individuals at a �me and place that meets their needs and interests.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>E7-E8.3 Organiza�on of Input and Public Par�cipa�on</head><p>The following text describes different teams that had responsibility for the 2025 WWAP, groups that had public par�cipa�on opportuni�es and how the two interacted to complete the WWAP revision and con�nue its implementa�on.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>WWAP 2025-2035 Coordina�on Team</head><p>The WWAP Coordina�on Team is a small group responsible for revision of the WWAP and mee�ng content requirements, including communica�ons, scheduling, coordina�ng Team tasks, and coordina�ng input from the public. The Team is made up of the following members made up of WDNR-Bureau of Natural Heritage Conserva�on (NHC) staff:</p><p>• Supervisors (two) from the Species Management Sec�on of the NHC program -Provided guidance on related Department policy and guidance, decisions on content and presenta�on </p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>SGCN Taxon Teams</head><p>The SGCN Taxon teams are made up of WDNR-Bureau of NHC staff. They are a permanent part of NHC's structure based on taxonomic groups: birds, herp�les (amphibians and rep�les), mammals, invertebrates and fish. The invertebrate group includes subgroups based on aqua�c and terrestrial invertebrate exper�se. Plants (vascular, non-vascular, lichens) are part of the Natural Community and Plants Team (below).</p><p>Individuals with expert and professional knowledge of our state natural communi�es or plant and animal species that are rare, declining, uncommon or unknown provide cri�cal and analy�cal input on biological and ecological issues, which is needed to iden�fy Species of Greatest Conserva�on Need (SGCN) as well as Species with Informa�on Needs (SGIN). Taxon Teams were responsible for upda�ng ranks that supported the revised SGCN/SGIN list, and assigna�on of associa�on scores. The approach used for these tasks, including public input, are described in detail in the methods for Elements 1 (sec�on E1.2) and 2 (sec�on E2.2). They analyzed and incorporated relevant species guidance, plans and databases into these major WWAP elements. They were direct contacts for public input into these tasks and incorporated this input into their decisions. Monitoring the status of SGCN is a core responsibility of the Taxon Teams and NHC, so updates occur independently of the 2025 WWAP.</p><p>Some Teams held a mul�ple day mee�ng to assess species in their taxa using the NatureServe Rank Calculator. Other teams met virtually to run the calculator, and others split up the list of species in their taxa and used the calculator individually. Therea�er, each Team met or communicated periodically over the course of approximately 18 months to incorporate and respond to public input, update the SGCN list, assign associa�on scores and develop conserva�on ac�ons for corresponding threats, including informa�on and research needs.</p><p>The Taxon Teams con�nue their work with the public during WWAP implementa�on. They provide technical consulta�on and support for conserva�on projects carried out by the Department and external en��es. This includes coordina�on of community-based monitoring that targets SGCN and their habitat and the correct applica�on of monitoring and survey protocols.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>Natural Community and Plants Team</head><p>The Natural Community and Plants Team provides cri�cal and analy�cal input on biological and ecological issues related to plants, natural communi�es and SGCN habitat. Its role regarding Plant SGCN is the same as the other SGCN Taxon Teams. This Team considers and integrates other plans and databases relevant to Natural Communi�es and habitats. They are primarily responsible for determining the Natural Community Types and Groups that were used to assess SGCN habitats. The natural community list is a core responsibility of this Team and NHC, so updates or improvements occur independently of the 2025 WWAP.</p><p>This Team was consulted by the Taxon Teams par�cularly for understanding SGCN and natural community (habitat). This Team updated plant state ranks, which culminated with the SGCN/SGIN added to the 2025 WWAP. They used the same approach to public input as the other Taxon Teams.</p><p>The Team also assigned the Natural Community (NC) x Ecological Landscape (EL) associa�on scores and updated COA atributes. The Natural Community and Plants Team is divided into working groups for specific issues or natural community groups (e.g., northern forest working group, climate vulnerability assessments for each community group). This work was integrated into the Natural Community Threats and Ac�ons Reports for Elements 3 and 4 of the WWAP.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>Other DNR Staff</head><p>Responsibility for development and implementa�on of the WWAP resides in the Bureau of Natural Heritage Conserva�on, which is part of the Division of Fish Wildlife and Parks. The Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources is large, with over 4,000 employees and diverse programs and responsibili�es. In Wisconsin, wildlife and game management, rare species conserva�on and enforcement, are merged into this single Department. This means public par�cipa�on includes a large cohort outside of the Teams described above, but within the Department. Communica�ons to other Department staff were provided for each major element of the WWAP revision. This was done through large email distribu�ons, the WWAP webpage and the Department's internal weekly newsleter, The Resource.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>External Partners</head><p>External partners included conserva�on organiza�ons, experts and organiza�ons focused on resource management and use sectors that affect SGCNs and their habitat, academic researchers, federal and state agency representa�ves. Partners were iden�fied from previous WWAP par�cipants and groups who could benefit from the WWAP or do conserva�on work in Wisconsin</p><p>To update SGCN/SGIN lists and associa�on scores, SGCN Taxon Teams iden�fied "experts" in each taxon that included other DNR staff and external partners with knowledge about the species (e.g., distribu�on and abundance) and their habitat. People on this list were iden�fied from the cumula�ve experience of the Taxon Team members and their interac�ons with colleagues, researchers, community scien�sts, etc.</p><p>Those individuals received an ini�al email linked to a par�cipa�on survey asking if and how they wanted to par�cipate: no par�cipa�on; contacted directly with review content; or remain on the email distribu�on list to receive content for each step in the WWAP revision process. The list was flexible in that people could self-iden�fy as having knowledge in other taxa and suggest other people for technical review. They were also encouraged to forward the email to others. Although all these requests were publicly posted on the WWAP webpage, this group was contacted directly on three occasions:</p><p>• Informa�on about rarity, trends and threats to assist with ranking • Review of proposed SRanks and SGCN/SGIN list • Review of SGCN x NC and SGCN x EL associa�on scores Teams followed up on each comment with addi�onal ques�ons and conversa�ons as needed.</p><p>Each comment received a response. All input and responses were documented.</p><p>When the Threats and Ac�ons Reports were posted for comment the full list of partners was included (i.e., not just those who had knowledge of par�cular species and habitat).</p><p>The en�re list of partners called upon to assist the Taxon Teams during development of Elements 1 to 4 was extensive, approximately 230 individuals and 30 organiza�ons, represen�ng a range of WWAP users as well as contributors to the data and informa�on presented in the WWAP. People from USDA and USGS were included among the species experts and partners.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>Tribal Engagement</head><p>The Coordina�on Team contacted the environmental or natural resource leaders for each of Wisconsin's eleven Federally recognized Tribal Na�ons and biological services staff from the Great Lakes Indian Fish and Wildlife Commission. One virtual introductory mee�ng was held in July 2023 and therea�er we conducted individual communica�ons and updates, including no�fica�on of all subsequent public par�cipa�on events described above for external partners.</p><p>Communica�ons were primarily through email and phone communica�ons with some in person and virtual mee�ngs.</p><p>Understandably, interest and feedback during the revision period were very limited. Our online research tells us most Tribal Na�ons have an environmental, natural resource or conserva�on program that reflects tribal interests and concerns. We believe a good approach during implementa�on is to con�nue mee�ng with and listening to interests and concerns of each Tribe and iden�fy key areas of commonality, offering the flexibility to bring this knowledge into the WWAP at any �me. We are grateful to members of the Great Lakes Indian Fish and Wildlife Commission who shared their knowledge and �me to help us get started.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>Web Pos�ngs and Schedule for Public Input</head><p>All dra� content was posted to the WWAP webpage that was publicly available throughout the revision and where people had opportuni�es to share knowledge during development of the 2025-2035 WWAP elements. The links to the content, input schedule and descrip�on of each dra� element are provided below. A schedule was posted for par�cipants to expect pos�ngs of dra� content and to show a �meline of the revision (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">E7-8.1</ref>). Although instruc�ons with each pos�ng had a deadline to make the best use of the comments, the op�ons remained open, and input was accepted at any �me. Alert banners were also added to the secondary pages for each of the major elements (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">E7</ref>-8.2).</p><p>Largely we provided opportunity to par�cipate in the revision through email and by pos�ng updates to our website, par�cularly for the first four elements that produced major outcomes for the 2025 WWAP. Experience with previous itera�ons, indicates this is what people are the most interested in.  </p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>Table E7-E8.2 Alert Banner for Input Opportuni�es Posted throughout the WWAP Revision</head><p>The last item in this schedule is a 14-day public review period to ensure people who have not been captured by previous communica�ons or would like to make addi�onal comments can do All commentors will receive a direct response to confirm how their input will be considered and for clarifica�on, if needed.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>Wisconsin Natural Resources Board</head><p>The Wisconsin Natural Resources Board (NRB or Board) sets policy for the Department of Natural Resources and exercises authority and responsibility in accordance with governing state laws. The public is welcome to atend any Natural Resources Board mee�ng unless it is no�ced as a closed or execu�ve session. Ci�zens may appear at Board mee�ngs or submit writen comments about issues that come before the Board. The Wisconsin Wildlife Ac�on Plan is brought to the NRB as an informa�on item.</p></div>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><head>Partner and Public Involvement during Implementa�on</head><p>The Bureau of Natural Heritage Conserva�on, which helps protect and restore Wisconsin's rare plants, animals and state natural areas, has primary responsibility for implemen�ng the WWAP. It engages with partners and the public to implement the WWAP in many ways: State Natural Area volunteer program that maintains and improves SGCN habitat 1 ; partnering on grants with federal, state and local agencies and organiza�ons; community monitoring to collect observa�ons of SGCN 2 ; and educa�onal field trips with the Natural Resources Founda�on of Wisconsin 3 to improve understanding of important SGCN habitats and the challenges they face.</p><p>PUBL-NH-951(E7-E8) 2025</p><p>1 <ref type="url" target="https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/">https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/</ref>, search "volunteer with DNR" 2 <ref type="url" target="https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/">https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/</ref>, search "citizen-based monitoring"</p><p>3 <ref type="url" target="https://www.wisconservation.org/">https://www.wisconservation.org/</ref> </p></div><figure xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xml:id="fig_0"><head>•</head><label></label><figDesc>Database and web lead -Collected, managed and organized the data and informa�on used to revise Elements 1 and 2 (e.g., NHI database); compila�on of COA atributes; development and implementa�on of WWAP webpage. • WWAP Coordinator -Communica�on with other states, overall organiza�on and fulfillment of WWAP requirements and submital, coordina�on with Taxon Teams. • WWAP Assistant -Monitored and facilitated communica�ons and instruc�ons to Taxon Teams and the public for comple�ng and reviewing Elements 1-4. This Team collec�vely defined the scope of each Element's revision, developed guidance and facilitated major elements of the WWAP carried out by the Taxon and Natural Community and Plants Teams for iden�fica�on of SGCN, associa�on scores, threats and ac�ons reports, and updates to Conserva�on Opportunity Areas (COA) atributes. It carried out all internal and external communica�ons for both technical and non-technical review with the public. It was assembled in May 2022 and will con�nue to work on updates and improvements a�er the 2025 WWAP is submited.</figDesc></figure>
